</span></div></div>\n<p><span style=\"display:none\" id=\"dynamic&#95;layout&#95;overrider\">Layout 2</span>\n</p>\n<div class=\"prp-pages-output\" lang=\"en\">\n<p><span><span class=\"pagenum ws-pagenum\" id=\"850\" data-page-number=\"850\" data-page-name=\"Page:Eminent Chinese Of The Ch\u2019ing Period - Hummel - 1943 - Vol. 2.pdf/248\" data-page-index=\"248\" data-page-quality=\"3\" title=\"Page:Eminent&#95;Chinese&#95;Of&#95;The&#95;Ch%E2%80%99ing&#95;Period&#95;-&#95;Hummel&#95;-&#95;1943&#95;-&#95;Vol.&#95;2.pdf/248\"><span id=\"pageindex&#95;248\" class=\"pagenum-inner ws-noexport\">&#8203;</span></span></span><b>WEI</b> Li \u9b4f\u79ae (<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r14767791\">.mw-parser-output .wst-tooltip{cursor:help;border-bottom:thin dotted cornflowerblue}.mw-parser-output .wst-tooltip-nodash{border-bottom:none}</style><span class=\"wst-tooltip wst-tooltip-dash\" title=\"Courtesy name (Tz\u00fb/Zi, \u5b57)\">T.</span>&#160;\u548c\u516c,&#32;<link rel=\"mw-deduplicated-inline-style\" href=\"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r14767791\" /><span class=\"wst-tooltip wst-tooltip-dash\" title=\"Literary name (Hao, \u865f)\">H.</span>&#160;\u543e\u5eec, \u5b63\u5b50), 1629\u20131695, poet, a native of Ning-tu, Kiangsi, was a younger brother of <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Wei_Hsi\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Wei Hsi\">Wei Hsi</a></span> and <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Wei_Chi-jui\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Wei Chi-jui\">Wei Chi-jui</a></span>&#32;[<i>qq.&#8239;v.</i>] and, like them, achieved fame as a writer. After 1659 he traveled extensively, from the Great Wall in the north to the Island of Hainan in the south, making friends of distinguished scholars whom he met. His two sons, Wei Shih-hsiao \u9b4f\u4e16\u509a (<link rel=\"mw-deduplicated-inline-style\" href=\"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r14767791\" /><span class=\"wst-tooltip wst-tooltip-dash\" title=\"Courtesy name (Tz\u00fb/Zi, \u5b57)\">T.</span>&#160;\u662d\u58eb,&#32;<link rel=\"mw-deduplicated-inline-style\" href=\"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r14767791\" /><span class=\"wst-tooltip wst-tooltip-dash\" title=\"Literary name (Hao, \u865f)\">H.</span>&#160;\u8015\u5ee1,&#32;b. 1659) and Wei Shih-yen \u9b4f\u4e16\u66e6 (<link rel=\"mw-deduplicated-inline-style\" href=\"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r14767791\" /><span class=\"wst-tooltip wst-tooltip-dash\" title=\"Courtesy name (Tz\u00fb/Zi, \u5b57)\">T.</span>&#160;\u656c\u58eb,&#32;<link rel=\"mw-deduplicated-inline-style\" href=\"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r14767791\" /><span class=\"wst-tooltip wst-tooltip-dash\" title=\"Literary name (Hao, \u865f)\">H.</span>&#160;\u7232\u8c37,&#32;b. 1662), and their cousin, Wei Shih-chieh (see under <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Wei_Chi-jui\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Wei Chi-jui\">Wei Chi-jui</a></span>), were together known as the \"Three Younger Wei\" (\u5c0f\u4e09\u9b4f). A third son, Wei Shih-k'an (see under <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Wei_Hsi\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Wei Hsi\">Wei Hsi</a></span>), was adopted by the latter, Wei Li made the acquaintance of the poet-statesman, <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Wang_Shih-ch%C3%AAn\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Wang Shih-ch\u00ean\">Wang Shih-ch\u00ean</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>]. They met at Canton in 1685 when the latter was commissioned to offer sacrifices to the South Seas. The prose and poetic works of Wei Li were assembled, chiefly by his brother, Wei Hsi, in 1671 and were published in 1679 in the <i>San Wei chi</i> (see under <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Wei_Hsi\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Wei Hsi\">Wei Hsi</a></span>) under the title <i>Wei Chi-tz\u016d w\u00ean-chi</i>, 16 <i>ch\u00fcan</i>. <i>Ch\u00fcan</i> 15 of this work comprises biographical sketches of some fifty \neminent men and women of Ning-tu, his native place.\n</p>\n<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r15431518\">.mw-parser-output .wst-rule{background-color:currentcolor;color:inherit;width:auto;margin:2px auto 2px auto;height:1px;overflow:hidden}.mw-parser-output .wst-rule.wst-rule-left{margin-left:0}.mw-parser-output .wst-rule.wst-rule-right{margin-right:0}.mw-parser-output .wst-rule.__uirule{background-color:#a2a9b1;color:inherit;border:0;margin-top:0.2em;margin-bottom:0.2em}</style><hr class=\"wst-rule wst-rule-left\" style=\"color:inherit;width:40%;\" />\n<p>[See bibliography for <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Wei_Hsi\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Wei Hsi\">Wei Hsi</a></span>, in particular <i>Wei Shu-tz\u016d w\u00ean-chi</i> 8/81b, 11/72a; <i>Wei-ku w\u00ean-kao</i> 8/3b.]\n</p>\n<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r15431519\">.mw-parser-output .wst-right{text-align:right}</style><div class=\"wst-right\" style=\"margin-right:2em;\">\n<p><span class=\"smallcaps\" style=\"font-variant:small-caps;\">Ts\u00eang Mien</span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<p><br />\n&#32;\n</p>\n</div>\n</div>"},"links":[{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Wang Shih-ch\u00ean"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Wei Chi-jui"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Wei Hsi"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Wei I-chieh"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Wei Y\u00fcan"},{"ns":102,"exists":"","*":"Author:Arthur W. Hummel"},{"ns":102,"exists":"","*":"Author:Ts\u00eang Mien"}],"sections":[],"wikitext":{"*":"{{Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period\n | volume   = 2\n | onlysection = wei li\n | previous = Wei I-chieh\n | next     = Wei Y\u00fcan\n | from     = 248\n | to       = 248\n | notes    =\n | section_author = Ts\u00eang Mien\n}}{{DEFAULTSORT:Wei Li}}"}}}